00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:13.940
friction in an organizations.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:17.380
And what I wanted to do was to
take a little bit of your time,

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:22.060
present some simple facts about
friction to set the stage.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:24.500
And then what we will do is, Bob and

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:27.420
I will have a conversation
about what causes friction.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:30.360
What are some ways maybe to mitigate them?

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:33.540
We hope to hear from you about your tools,
techniques,

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:36.210
ideas that you might have as well.

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:39.580
First, friction in organizations.

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:44.310
We all have visceral
understandings of friction.

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:46.720
When there is friction, there is noise.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:48.540
Sometimes there is heat.

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:50.350
There is grinding.

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:54.450
There is also frustration at
an emotional level, and maybe fatigue.

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:58.830
But from the individual point of view,
let's give you some data on drag and

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:00.770
friction in organizations.

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:06.288
One study by Rob Crossman and his
colleagues found knowledge workers spend

00:04:06.288 --> 00:04:11.350
80% to 85% their time attending
meetings and responding to requests.

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:17.830
Instantly, that tells you where
friction can lie right there.

00:04:17.830 --> 00:04:20.380
In the same study,
they found that leaders,

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:24.890
when they delegate things to their
subordinates, they underestimate by as

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:32.500
much as 50% the collaboration burdens we
actually place on their subordinates.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:35.450
So, delegation isn't simply delegation.

00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:38.390
You're also placing coordination
burdens on your subordinates.

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:44.380
And another form of friction,
of course, I guess is email, and

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:51.470
one other study of a mid-size firm showed
that if you assume, it cost 95 cents

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:56.770
to process an email for this medium
size firm, it costs a million dollars.

00:04:58.390 --> 00:05:00.975
Let's turn to the organizational view.

00:05:00.975 --> 00:05:08.980
[NOISE] Let's actually turn to
the organizational view in just a minute.

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:10.720
&gt;&gt; Getting some adult supervision here.

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:12.150
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:12.650
Yeah.

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:17.780
So, in organizations, friction often
is manifested as interruptions.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:21.551
In one ethnographic study by Mark and

00:05:21.551 --> 00:05:26.540
colleagues, they followed 36
executives for half a day.

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:30.790
And stunningly, they found the average
amount of time an executive spent on

00:05:30.790 --> 00:05:35.670
a task before being interrupted was
three minutes and five seconds.

00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:39.110
In another study,
people spent 12 minutes and

00:05:39.110 --> 00:05:42.050
18 seconds on a work
domain before switching.

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:47.590
And surprisingly, people interrupted
themselves 44% of the time,

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:55.670
often in response to a question,
a request or a proposal by somebody.

00:05:55.670 --> 00:06:00.270
One other problem of friction is
something called attention residue.

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:06.184
The psychologist Leroy and his colleagues
found that when people move from one

00:06:06.184 --> 00:06:11.930
task to another, the previous task
has conceptual or cognitive overhang.

00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:15.980
And you actually have to unburden
yourself of that overhang before

00:06:15.980 --> 00:06:18.460
you concentrate on the new task.

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:24.600
And all of that actually
depletes ability and performance.

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:28.530
And finally,
the organizational effects of friction.

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:30.430
The more friction in organizations,

00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:34.810
employees might quit because
of damaged work life balance.

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:39.710
There's also, speed is impaired,
and we lose agility and speed.

00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:44.750
And we, of course,
don't get the opportunity to innovate or

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:50.030
do something called deep work that
requires extraordinary concentration.

00:06:50.030 --> 00:06:53.900
So, this is really the background
about friction and drag.

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:59.170
And just to let you know, we're going to
use both of these terms interchangeably.

00:06:59.170 --> 00:07:02.580
Some of you might, in the questions,
suggest there are differences.

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:07.050
And we, we're very open to what
those differences indeed are.

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:12.880
So, let me quickly, without further ado,
invite Bob into the conversation.

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:19.480
Bob, when normally one thinks of friction,
one usually thinks of persons.

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:21.370
And normally, one thinks of ****.

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:25.455
Is that the only source of
friction in your organization?

00:07:25.455 --> 00:07:28.030
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Or are there other sources?

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:31.718
&gt;&gt; Well, for those of you who don't know,
for better or worse.

00:07:31.718 --> 00:07:35.229
The reason Huggy's asking this,
I wrote one, and

00:07:35.229 --> 00:07:41.040
I now I'm writing another book about
the problems with **** in organizations.

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:47.690
But I think actually, that's too crude
a term [LAUGH] in a couple of ways.

00:07:47.690 --> 00:07:50.380
And to me, there's two parts of this.

00:07:50.380 --> 00:07:54.460
First of all, what we're doing, and

00:07:54.460 --> 00:07:58.650
what we're trying to do is to
really focus on not just friction

00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:03.220
as something you do to yourself, and all
of us, maybe we could be more efficient.

00:08:03.220 --> 00:08:05.810
I mean,
there's Is books on like essentialism and

00:08:05.810 --> 00:08:10.370
how if you just managed yourself better
you'd have less friction in your life but

00:08:10.370 --> 00:08:13.720
we're real interested in how groups and
organizations cause friction.

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:19.121
And we'll keep talking about that and we
would love your ideas about the causes and

00:08:19.121 --> 00:08:19.717
the cures.

00:08:19.717 --> 00:08:25.111
But on the **** thing,
there are particular sorts of people and

00:08:25.111 --> 00:08:30.118
they may, or may, not be ****,
that I think are especially

00:08:30.118 --> 00:08:36.050
prone to cause trouble in organization
when it comes to friction.

00:08:36.050 --> 00:08:39.950
And then, there are people who
are especially prone to get rid of it.

00:08:39.950 --> 00:08:43.110
And this is one thing that we've been
noticing in the various organizations,

00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:46.070
including the company
Stanford that we work at.

00:08:46.070 --> 00:08:50.550
That you will have two people
in very similar positions

00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:54.950
who they magically under the same set
of rules, the same set of constraints,

00:08:54.950 --> 00:08:58.460
they find ways to clear the way and
things are easy to do.

00:08:58.460 --> 00:09:03.750
And then, there are other people who for
them personally it's difficult to do it.

00:09:03.750 --> 00:09:07.290
To get things done, and when you go to
them and ask for help, they make things so

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:12.350
complicated that everybody around
them gets dragged into this vortex.

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:17.091
And you can sort of say that there
are some people who are like WD40 and

00:09:17.091 --> 00:09:21.998
some people who are throwing sand,
and there is some specific research

00:09:21.998 --> 00:09:25.784
that might be relevant on
toxic people who add friction.

00:09:25.784 --> 00:09:30.257
A researcher from New York University
actually called these Rule Nazis and

00:09:30.257 --> 00:09:33.878
these are the kind of people,
and we have them at Stanford and

00:09:33.878 --> 00:09:37.605
every large bureaucracy I've
ever worked with has them.

00:09:37.605 --> 00:09:41.360
They are people who,
when you go to them with any problem and

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:44.970
they get seven times more complicated
then it perhaps needs to be.

00:09:46.410 --> 00:09:49.130
They pull in all sorts of people,
they are constantly worry about

00:09:49.130 --> 00:09:52.220
everything goes wrong so it takes
nine times longer to get things done.

00:09:52.220 --> 00:09:54.910
And one of the things
we're interested in is

00:09:54.910 --> 00:09:58.256
what are the situations that attract and
breed Rule Nazis.

00:09:58.256 --> 00:10:02.060
Because that behavior actually might
be because they're scrutinized so

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:04.200
closely by the system
they make the mistake.

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:05.540
They just get slammed.

00:10:05.540 --> 00:10:07.285
It may not be a personality variable.

00:10:07.285 --> 00:10:12.013
And then, what are the of hallmarks of
people in systems that are able to get

00:10:12.013 --> 00:10:14.450
around and do things easily.

00:10:14.450 --> 00:10:17.290
So there's a whole bunch of other
organizational causes we might want to

00:10:17.290 --> 00:10:17.816
talk about.

00:10:17.816 --> 00:10:21.841
But one of the things we're interested
in is, so what is the difference between

00:10:21.841 --> 00:10:25.747
people in groups who seem to easily get
things done, and remove friction from

00:10:25.747 --> 00:10:29.850
the same system, same set of constraints
versus making everything difficult?

00:10:31.150 --> 00:10:33.640
&gt;&gt; I love the idea of the Rule Nazi.

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:39.169
I hope all of you can, of course,
quickly search your memories and

00:10:39.169 --> 00:10:42.343
identify the Rule Nazis in your life.

00:10:42.343 --> 00:10:47.173
I'm curious, Bob, since you mentioned
the two individuals you know,

00:10:47.173 --> 00:10:51.355
one kind of person clears the way and
the other person kind of follows rules,

00:10:51.355 --> 00:10:54.890
would you think that
the psychological characteristic of

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:59.170
openness has anything to do with
whether anyone's a Rule Nazi or not?

00:10:59.170 --> 00:11:02.610
So in other words,
are open minded people less of Rule Nazis?

00:11:02.610 --> 00:11:07.610
&gt;&gt; Well there’s openness,
I also think that with at least, and

00:11:07.610 --> 00:11:11.460
Heather Halverson is the one who
did this research by the way.

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:14.918
That people to use this
cool word Rule Nazis,

00:11:14.918 --> 00:11:19.468
people who tend to be so
anxious about making a mistake, and

00:11:19.468 --> 00:11:25.440
perhaps have been slapped down so
severely for making a small mistake.

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:29.450
They also tend to be
quite anxiety-prone too.

00:11:29.450 --> 00:11:33.994
That that's the way Now, their kind of
person might become petty dictators.

00:11:33.994 --> 00:11:40.820
Sort of the way they gain control of
others is by throwing up difficulties.

00:11:40.820 --> 00:11:45.186
To move away from **** and
negative personality types.

00:11:45.186 --> 00:11:49.957
One of the things that I'd be curious from
some of the people who are watching this

00:11:49.957 --> 00:11:53.833
to hear if this is something that
comes up in your organizations.

00:11:53.833 --> 00:11:57.920
I actually think that, although it's
always interesting to talk about

00:11:57.920 --> 00:12:01.203
personalities and
they're always very big in our world,

00:12:01.203 --> 00:12:05.893
some of the things that happen in
organizations that cause friction we're

00:12:05.893 --> 00:12:11.250
both interested in, or when they create
incentives for people to add friction.

00:12:11.250 --> 00:12:15.510
So one of the things that we've noticed
at a number of organizations that we have

00:12:15.510 --> 00:12:21.480
worked with is there'll be all sorts
of incentives for you to add a program.

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:25.830
So that if you have a new program,
a new idea, a new initiative.

00:12:25.830 --> 00:12:27.330
But you get glory for it.

00:12:28.810 --> 00:12:31.810
And that everybody at our
organization gets glory for doing it.

00:12:31.810 --> 00:12:33.080
Even if you just sent emails.

00:12:33.080 --> 00:12:37.450
Like it might create the appearance or
the actual reality that you're doing work.

00:12:37.450 --> 00:12:40.893
And the more people you send it to, the
more people know that you're doing work.

00:12:40.893 --> 00:12:44.204
But there's this thing,
and it's sort of like the,

00:12:44.204 --> 00:12:47.920
in economics they call it
the tragedy of the commons.

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:51.730
Were the incentives that help you
individually get ahead in your career and

00:12:51.730 --> 00:12:56.120
make a mark and be known,
it just adds more and more and more load?

00:12:56.120 --> 00:13:00.070
At Stanford, just for example,
we have a new set of teaching

00:13:01.540 --> 00:13:07.540
evaluations at Stanford, and the committee
on it worked on it very enthusiastically,

00:13:07.540 --> 00:13:10.090
and so what they did is
the created a situation where,

00:13:10.090 --> 00:13:15.060
wow, I've gotta spend a couple
more hours a year dealing with it.

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:17.230
I had to go to a meeting
to learn about it.

00:13:17.230 --> 00:13:21.352
And I honestly don't think the new system
is any better than the last system, but

00:13:21.352 --> 00:13:24.334
all the people involved in doing it,
they get more glory.

00:13:24.334 --> 00:13:25.430
So I guess that's good.

00:13:26.840 --> 00:13:30.370
So that's one kind of incentive where
everybody has incentives to add, but

00:13:30.370 --> 00:13:31.700
not to subtract.

00:13:31.700 --> 00:13:35.210
And we all suffer from the weight
of more and more and more programs.

00:13:35.210 --> 00:13:38.760
And the other thing is, I don't know
what it's like in your organization, but

00:13:38.760 --> 00:13:43.500
in many organizations that I work with,
the amount of pay grade that you have,

00:13:43.500 --> 00:13:47.070
your levels of pay grade, this is
certainly true in the civil service.

00:13:47.070 --> 00:13:49.999
The more people report to you,
the more you get paid.

00:13:49.999 --> 00:13:54.400
So there's all these incentive for
people to build kingdoms.

00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:58.803
And the way that adds friction is when you
have more people, you have sort of more

00:13:58.803 --> 00:14:02.830
administrators and maybe more faculty
members or more direct workers.

00:14:02.830 --> 00:14:08.820
Who add more and more load on one another
because size almost always adds friction.

00:14:08.820 --> 00:14:12.680
So although I think there
are some personality types

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:18.870
where my mind tends to go
with this problem is what

00:14:18.870 --> 00:14:23.890
are the incentives in the organization,
that unwillingly cause friction and

00:14:23.890 --> 00:14:27.910
one of them is a, each one of us when we
throw something out we get reward and

00:14:27.910 --> 00:14:30.740
nobody gets rewarded for
getting rid of something.

00:14:30.740 --> 00:14:33.528
And then,
the second one is this idea of sizes and

00:14:33.528 --> 00:14:38.016
this is a big thing that our books getting
up excellence which is Which is size,

00:14:38.016 --> 00:14:42.504
is almost always causes more friction and
bad interpersonal relationships,

00:14:42.504 --> 00:14:44.626
the more people you add to a problem.

00:14:44.626 --> 00:14:49.170
&gt;&gt; I think what's kind of very
striking is Bob has identified one of

00:14:49.170 --> 00:14:54.695
the important Is that leads to friction,
and that is incentive.

00:14:54.695 --> 00:14:57.940
And I very much like your point, Bob.

00:14:57.940 --> 00:15:02.320
That the incentives are asymmetric,
i.e., you're rewarded for

00:15:02.320 --> 00:15:04.610
adding, but not for subtract.

00:15:04.610 --> 00:15:06.950
Adjunct being the case in point.

00:15:06.950 --> 00:15:14.490
But the way I also thought you described
the tragedy of the attentional condenses.

00:15:14.490 --> 00:15:18.620
Perhaps when one copies everybody
indiscriminently on the mean,

00:15:18.620 --> 00:15:22.560
is that like another example of something?

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:25.430
Damaging the commons,
the attention on commons.

00:15:25.430 --> 00:15:31.010
Now, Bob and I went into an executive
program recently with one C as you recall.

00:15:31.010 --> 00:15:37.067
Said that the person who actually
kind of copied me on an email.

00:15:37.067 --> 00:15:40.094
If they found out that
person is going to be fired.

00:15:40.094 --> 00:15:44.445
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] Are punishments the only

00:15:44.445 --> 00:15:49.362
way to deal with damaging
the intentional comments, or

00:15:49.362 --> 00:15:52.930
are there other more mischievous ways?

00:15:52.930 --> 00:15:55.740
&gt;&gt; Well, I don't know if there
are more mischievous ways, but

00:15:55.740 --> 00:16:00.420
certainly at least removing incentives for

00:16:00.420 --> 00:16:05.840
people to add friction and
giving them incentives for

00:16:05.840 --> 00:16:10.710
them to get rid of it at the very
least is actually Not a bad idea.

00:16:10.710 --> 00:16:13.590
Another organizational design feature,
and this is controversial.

00:16:13.590 --> 00:16:17.310
So I'll say this and see if we get some
reactions out of folks is everybody talks

00:16:17.310 --> 00:16:20.610
about how great transparency is,
and how all we gotta share,

00:16:20.610 --> 00:16:22.180
we gotta tell everybody what we're doing.

00:16:22.180 --> 00:16:27.140
Well, there is an argument that I don't
need to know everything you're doing and

00:16:27.140 --> 00:16:28.490
you don't need to know
everything I'm doing.

00:16:28.490 --> 00:16:32.650
In fact, it just overloads me with
literally more junk on my mind and

00:16:32.650 --> 00:16:35.750
the more that's on my mind
the worse I do on my job.

00:16:35.750 --> 00:16:41.920
And if you go to the extreme case to
the most secretive organization on earth,

00:16:41.920 --> 00:16:45.320
it's not the CIA, it's Apple computer,
at least that I know of.

00:16:47.390 --> 00:16:50.910
And there's an interesting argument and
Huggy and I have talked to insiders and

00:16:50.910 --> 00:16:53.860
of course if we used his name they
would have to shoot us, because,

00:16:53.860 --> 00:16:58.290
it's much easier to get information
about the CIA as we've seen or

00:16:58.290 --> 00:17:00.200
with the NSA with all the leaks and
everything.

00:17:00.200 --> 00:17:02.780
But Apple, there's been no breach yet, and

00:17:03.960 --> 00:17:09.450
one of the things that we know about Apple
very well is they are extremely secretive.

00:17:09.450 --> 00:17:14.505
And outside of the small,
immediate team you work on,

00:17:14.505 --> 00:17:19.635
nobody is allowed to ask anybody,
to know anything, their employees badges

00:17:19.635 --> 00:17:24.555
only let them in a very small part of
the organization even quiet senior people.

00:17:24.555 --> 00:17:29.045
And although there are some
disadvantages to that level of secrecy.

00:17:29.045 --> 00:17:30.795
We could talk about those, I mean Apple,

00:17:30.795 --> 00:17:33.905
aren't they the largest most successful
company in the world right now.

00:17:33.905 --> 00:17:36.650
I think they're back to number one,

00:17:36.650 --> 00:17:40.450
it always makes me wonder there's some
coordination issues and the like, but

00:17:40.450 --> 00:17:44.370
to me that shows the positive
advantage of secrecy.

00:17:44.370 --> 00:17:47.510
Because one of the reasons is,
they just have less cognitive load,

00:17:47.510 --> 00:17:49.320
they just have less friction.

00:17:49.320 --> 00:17:52.070
They just get to work on
whatever they're working on,

00:17:52.070 --> 00:17:55.670
and they can't even know what's
going on in the rest of the company.

00:17:55.670 --> 00:17:58.971
&gt;&gt; We have a related
question from the audience.

00:17:58.971 --> 00:18:01.010
&gt;&gt; Thank.
&gt;&gt; Also somewhat controversial.

00:18:01.010 --> 00:18:06.180
So one person is asking about
collaboration and teamwork.

00:18:06.180 --> 00:18:10.510
It's often a value that many companies and
managers hold very dear.

00:18:10.510 --> 00:18:13.040
But is that also potentially
a cause of friction or drag?

00:18:13.040 --> 00:18:15.647
&gt;&gt; Yeah, there's a real
interesting study about this,

00:18:15.647 --> 00:18:17.127
do you want to talk about that?

00:18:17.127 --> 00:18:17.782
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:18:17.782 --> 00:18:21.030
So who was the person
who asked the question.

00:18:21.030 --> 00:18:22.160
&gt;&gt; I don't know, I can find out.

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:24.860
&gt;&gt; Because that is
a really good question and

00:18:24.860 --> 00:18:26.310
we need to-
&gt;&gt; Karen.

00:18:28.580 --> 00:18:30.090
&gt;&gt; Karen, thank you for that question.

00:18:30.090 --> 00:18:30.750
&gt;&gt; Great question.

00:18:30.750 --> 00:18:33.600
&gt;&gt; That is a very good question.

00:18:33.600 --> 00:18:38.623
The short answer is, yes,
collaboration is a burden.

00:18:38.623 --> 00:18:42.579
And if you've forgotten
everything from this webinar and

00:18:42.579 --> 00:18:47.260
maybe you want to remember just sort
of one thing, and the one thing is

00:18:47.260 --> 00:18:53.100
think about how collaboration actually
burdens your best performers.

00:18:53.100 --> 00:18:59.270
For the sake of this argument let's assume
each one of you listening to our webinar,

00:18:59.270 --> 00:19:02.430
we'll assume that each one
of you has 100 subordinates.

00:19:02.430 --> 00:19:07.910
Let's further stipulate, out of the 100,
that 10 are at the very top.

00:19:07.910 --> 00:19:11.270
Now, in general and
I'm making a very gross generalization,

00:19:11.270 --> 00:19:16.510
please correct me Bob if I
strayed off the reservation here.

00:19:16.510 --> 00:19:20.610
On a general basis,
if you have ten high performers, one would

00:19:20.610 --> 00:19:25.840
assume that five of them, they're great
performers, but they're high maintenance.

00:19:25.840 --> 00:19:30.350
They want raises, they want recognition,
they want attention from you.

00:19:30.350 --> 00:19:34.164
Loosely we can call them [BLEEP] because
they're never likely to be grateful.

00:19:34.164 --> 00:19:35.760
&gt;&gt; That's sexist, but it's okay.

00:19:35.760 --> 00:19:36.370
&gt;&gt; I'm sorry.

00:19:36.370 --> 00:19:41.570
[LAUGH] Now, the other five are people

00:19:41.570 --> 00:19:45.850
Rob Cross and Adam Grant would
refer to as the extra milers.

00:19:45.850 --> 00:19:47.450
And that's the study-
&gt;&gt; The givers.

00:19:47.450 --> 00:19:49.860
&gt;&gt; The givers Bob was alluding to.

00:19:49.860 --> 00:19:53.000
So when you think of the extra milers,
this

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:58.026
is the other half of your top performers,
the remaining five in your top ten.

00:19:58.026 --> 00:20:03.090
These are not only your high performers
but they're also the most helpful people.

00:20:03.090 --> 00:20:07.990
They respond to everybody's requests for
collaboration and help and so forth.

00:20:07.990 --> 00:20:14.940
And it turns out in a couple of studies,
when they receive more than 24 requests

00:20:14.940 --> 00:20:19.770
in a week or so, they begin to harbor
thoughts of leaving the company.

00:20:19.770 --> 00:20:24.650
The extra milers, they're so
cooperative their main problems is that

00:20:24.650 --> 00:20:29.610
they don't know how to say no and
as a result they seem the most disengaged.

00:20:29.610 --> 00:20:35.610
Indeed, we might argue that for
many of you listening to this webinar,

00:20:35.610 --> 00:20:40.502
if you at the end of this webinar have any
new ideas, whom are you going to turn and

00:20:40.502 --> 00:20:43.190
entrust them to, your extra milers.

00:20:43.190 --> 00:20:46.493
It's very unlikely you're actually
going to ask your [BLEEP]

00:20:46.493 --> 00:20:50.670
to kind of help you out, you're going to
ask your extra milers to do this.

00:20:50.670 --> 00:20:54.154
And so what you're doing is
you're putting more friction and

00:20:54.154 --> 00:20:58.455
more collaboration burden on them while
they're the ones being burnt out.

00:20:58.455 --> 00:20:59.555
Do you agree with me?

00:20:59.555 --> 00:21:04.264
&gt;&gt; I agree with you but I would also go
back to the organizational consequences.

00:21:04.264 --> 00:21:08.701
Because the study which Adam Grant and
Rob Cross did is very interesting.

00:21:08.701 --> 00:21:12.914
because it's essentially showing that
there's a small percentage of people who

00:21:12.914 --> 00:21:16.218
take the lion's share of the burden for
collaboration, and

00:21:16.218 --> 00:21:18.840
they allow everybody else
to do what they want.

00:21:18.840 --> 00:21:23.225
And so givers like that give so much that
they burn out and it's really a risk,

00:21:23.225 --> 00:21:27.011
but I also go back to the organizational
causes and consequences.

00:21:27.011 --> 00:21:32.288
Because if you are in your team,
if you are a senior executive who

00:21:32.288 --> 00:21:37.960
is into organizational design and
incentives, if you reward both,

00:21:37.960 --> 00:21:43.630
let's call them the free riders
instead of using the “p” word,

00:21:43.630 --> 00:21:49.112
as much as you or more than you
reward the ones who are givers.

00:21:49.112 --> 00:21:53.060
Then there's something wrong with
who you're giving an incentive to,

00:21:53.060 --> 00:21:54.830
who you're promoting.

00:21:54.830 --> 00:21:58.960
So a lot of this goes back to
organizational process and

00:21:58.960 --> 00:22:04.240
we may blame the sort of people
who are doing the free riders,

00:22:04.240 --> 00:22:07.900
but if they've been rewarded for it,
if that's the kind of people you attract.

00:22:07.900 --> 00:22:10.890
If those are the kind of people you
promote it's actually rational behavior

00:22:10.890 --> 00:22:14.370
for you to do the work for me,
and to go to all the meetings and

00:22:14.370 --> 00:22:18.260
do all the collaboration, while I just
focus on building my star power and

00:22:18.260 --> 00:22:20.850
we get rewarded equally or me even more.

00:22:20.850 --> 00:22:23.860
It's partly an organizational issue too.

00:22:23.860 --> 00:22:28.830
&gt;&gt; But you know I hope this can
clarify your question, Karen.

00:22:28.830 --> 00:22:32.580
But the other thing, I just want to
quickly jump on what Bob mentioned about

00:22:32.580 --> 00:22:37.389
the organization side is, often,
when I work with executives and

00:22:37.389 --> 00:22:40.820
ask them, what do you think we
should do about the extra milers?

00:22:40.820 --> 00:22:45.726
And indeed I urge all of you to think of
giving a gift to the extra milers when

00:22:45.726 --> 00:22:46.610
you go back.

00:22:46.610 --> 00:22:50.449
Your own extra milers on whom
you heap cognitive load and

00:22:50.449 --> 00:22:53.140
the burdens of collaboration.

00:22:53.140 --> 00:22:57.450
Usually executives kind of think of
things like we've gotta work with them so

00:22:57.450 --> 00:22:58.920
that they delegate more.

00:23:00.890 --> 00:23:04.223
That seems sensible but the one
thing with extra milers we know is,

00:23:04.223 --> 00:23:08.092
if you take away anything they feel that
there's a reduction of their role and

00:23:08.092 --> 00:23:09.710
their importance.

00:23:09.710 --> 00:23:11.830
And they're not too happy with it.

00:23:11.830 --> 00:23:16.610
The other kinds of things that people come
up with are we'll give them a day off, or

00:23:16.610 --> 00:23:18.550
we'll give them an evening off.

00:23:18.550 --> 00:23:22.820
Instead, the things that we found
that help these extra milers,

00:23:22.820 --> 00:23:25.850
is not giving a day off,
because they all like to work.

00:23:25.850 --> 00:23:29.320
It's actually giving the day off,
free from interruption.

00:23:29.320 --> 00:23:32.110
That's the kind of reward that
we are actually thinking about.

00:23:32.110 --> 00:23:34.730
So you come to work,
you're doing your work but

00:23:34.730 --> 00:23:38.140
nobody's bothering you, firing a lot
of questions and asking you for

00:23:38.140 --> 00:23:42.365
help so that you harbor thoughts
of leaving the organization.

00:23:42.365 --> 00:23:45.548
So, did that help, Karen?

00:23:45.548 --> 00:23:46.756
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it seems so.

00:23:48.868 --> 00:23:54.682
She did ask, is there an optimal
mix of givers and free riders, etc.

00:23:56.150 --> 00:24:01.759
&gt;&gt; That's actually a much more difficult
kind of question to answer because

00:24:01.759 --> 00:24:07.980
the optimum mix of givers and takers is,
you can easily exhaust the supply of

00:24:07.980 --> 00:24:11.346
Givers and it's the number of takers-
&gt;&gt; They can tolerate.

00:24:11.346 --> 00:24:12.634
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Yeah.

00:24:12.634 --> 00:24:18.140
&gt;&gt; So you know, it is also interesting,
and this is, again, a more organizational

00:24:18.140 --> 00:24:24.235
design approach, that I don't know about
the optimal mix of givers and takers.

00:24:24.235 --> 00:24:28.865
Adam Grant, who wrote the famous book Give
and Take, wonderful guy, he would say

00:24:28.865 --> 00:24:33.995
the optimal mix is, how he implies it
is like 100% givers and zero takers.

00:24:33.995 --> 00:24:37.155
But life's not going to work that way,
I don't think, in the near future for

00:24:37.155 --> 00:24:37.855
any of us.

00:24:39.485 --> 00:24:45.610
But one of the mixed things that I think
also really affects friction which is just

00:24:45.610 --> 00:24:49.540
now occurring to me, but something that
Huggy and I have both thought a lot about,

00:24:49.540 --> 00:24:53.750
is the percentage of people who
are specialists versus generalists.

00:24:53.750 --> 00:24:58.866
Because, you know,
my wife who used to be a lawyer used

00:24:58.866 --> 00:25:05.890
to joke that a generalist is somebody
who knows everything about nothing.

00:25:11.710 --> 00:25:12.292
No, no no.

00:25:12.292 --> 00:25:13.330
Nothing about everything.

00:25:13.330 --> 00:25:17.580
And a specialist is someone who
knows everything about nothing.

00:25:17.580 --> 00:25:20.650
In organizations,
I think that the biggest risk,

00:25:20.650 --> 00:25:25.290
as opposed to givers and takers, is having
too many people who are generalists.

00:25:25.290 --> 00:25:27.170
And it's funny, because I think
I'm pretty much of a generalist.

00:25:27.170 --> 00:25:30.300
Because when you have people who are
generalists, what you have is a bunch of

00:25:30.300 --> 00:25:34.940
people who feel obligated to get in one
another's spaces and tell them what to do

00:25:34.940 --> 00:25:38.810
and what they ought to do, but actually
don't really know that much about it.

00:25:38.810 --> 00:25:42.790
So they do just enough to
insist on reports or new ideas.

00:25:42.790 --> 00:25:45.760
But they actually don't know
how to solve the problem.

00:25:47.050 --> 00:25:51.590
So my perspective I guess this let's us
get back to organizations like Apple.

00:25:51.590 --> 00:25:54.820
And I think of Netflix,
which I know a little bit,

00:25:54.820 --> 00:25:59.000
which actually has a lot of specialists
and is a well-run organization,

00:25:59.000 --> 00:26:03.070
is you need just enough generalists to
know how to glue everything together.

00:26:03.070 --> 00:26:05.260
But once you have excessive generalists,

00:26:05.260 --> 00:26:08.310
you start having people who throw
in friction because they think

00:26:08.310 --> 00:26:10.610
they know the answer to things that
they don't know the answer to.

00:26:10.610 --> 00:26:12.640
&gt;&gt; That's an excellent point, Bob.

00:26:12.640 --> 00:26:15.590
I mean, this idea of generalism and
specialism.

00:26:15.590 --> 00:26:19.582
I think it's not only incentives but
also the depth of capabilities.

00:26:19.582 --> 00:26:20.359
[CROSSTALK].

00:26:20.359 --> 00:26:21.552
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:26:21.552 --> 00:26:23.455
&gt;&gt; That's a really well taken point.

00:26:23.455 --> 00:26:25.250
I haven't thought about it.

00:26:25.250 --> 00:26:26.720
I'm also keeping track of time.

00:26:26.720 --> 00:26:30.430
We focused on incentives as
a source of drag and friction.

00:26:31.680 --> 00:26:33.600
What about ignorance?

00:26:33.600 --> 00:26:35.030
And here, and

00:26:35.030 --> 00:26:37.025
the reason I sort of ask about it is-
&gt;&gt; That gets you,

00:26:37.025 --> 00:26:37.980
that's related to generalists.

00:26:37.980 --> 00:26:38.539
That's interesting.

00:26:38.539 --> 00:26:42.470
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
that's exactly why I was touching on that.

00:26:42.470 --> 00:26:44.930
Because as you said,
the generalists were ignorant.

00:26:44.930 --> 00:26:47.880
But more broadly what
I was thinking of was,

00:26:47.880 --> 00:26:51.080
ignorance as a source of
friction in organizations.

00:26:51.080 --> 00:26:54.950
And I'm reminded of a study by our
wonderful colleagues Dale Miller and

00:26:54.950 --> 00:27:00.280
Deborah Prentice where what they did
was they asked Stanford undergrads,

00:27:00.280 --> 00:27:04.740
what is your private attitude
towards the consumption of alcohol?

00:27:04.740 --> 00:27:05.990
And, individually,

00:27:05.990 --> 00:27:10.660
Standford undergrads said they didn't
approve of drinking alcohol on campus.

00:27:10.660 --> 00:27:14.290
But, on the other hand, when they asked
the very same Standford students, what do

00:27:14.290 --> 00:27:20.095
you think is the attitude of the average
Standford student for consuming alcohol?

00:27:20.095 --> 00:27:25.140
Their view was that the average Stanford
student actually was very permissive

00:27:25.140 --> 00:27:28.121
in terms of his or
her tolerance of alcohol.

00:27:28.121 --> 00:27:32.693
And you, is that the kind of ignorance
one needs to worried about here?

00:27:32.693 --> 00:27:33.670
Here it's pluralistic-
&gt;&gt; It's pluralistic ignorance.

00:27:33.670 --> 00:27:38.642
Well, that's one kind of ignorance,
and certainly that's a problem,

00:27:38.642 --> 00:27:42.150
and obviously alcohol at
Stanford is a huge issue.

00:27:42.150 --> 00:27:44.308
You've been watching the news.

00:27:44.308 --> 00:27:50.030
But to me the worst sort of
ignorance is when ignorance and

00:27:50.030 --> 00:27:52.220
arrogance are brought together.

00:27:52.220 --> 00:27:54.490
And this is the classic
time when friction, and

00:27:54.490 --> 00:27:58.901
other horrible things
happen in organizations.

00:27:58.901 --> 00:28:02.630
So when powerful people think that
they know how everything works and

00:28:02.630 --> 00:28:05.270
then they come in and
start telling other people what to do.

00:28:05.270 --> 00:28:11.190
And you have that in your slide deck
where when people, when superiors

00:28:11.190 --> 00:28:16.200
assign jobs to subordinates, they tend
to underestimate how long it will take.

00:28:16.200 --> 00:28:18.860
Classic problem that happens.

00:28:18.860 --> 00:28:23.190
But yeah, certainly ignorance
is a cause of friction.

00:28:23.190 --> 00:28:28.023
And as with all management, and this is
something that I talk about in Good Boss,

00:28:28.023 --> 00:28:32.146
Bad Boss and talk about in a lot of
Stanford Executive Programs, is that

00:28:32.146 --> 00:28:37.050
the hallmark of a good boss, and this is
something that will reduce friction, and

00:28:37.050 --> 00:28:41.922
it's the opposite of ignorance, is knowing
what it feels like to work for them.

00:28:41.922 --> 00:28:46.217
And having enough empathy, sympathy and
understanding of the people that they

00:28:46.217 --> 00:28:50.057
lead, that they know when to get in
their face and tell them what to do and

00:28:50.057 --> 00:28:51.547
they know when to back off.

00:28:51.547 --> 00:28:54.326
Either because they don't need the help or
actually by getting in,

00:28:54.326 --> 00:28:56.010
they'll be making things worse.

00:28:56.010 --> 00:28:59.520
So this is one of those cases
where self-awareness is one of

00:28:59.520 --> 00:29:01.650
the cures to this malady.

00:29:01.650 --> 00:29:04.340
&gt;&gt; Right, and Bob's point about
self-awareness being a cure to

00:29:04.340 --> 00:29:05.870
the malady again is well taken.

00:29:05.870 --> 00:29:10.110
Because in this very same Stanford study
that both of us mentioned moments ago,

00:29:10.110 --> 00:29:11.720
what was the solution?

00:29:12.780 --> 00:29:16.140
Clearly, individual students
thought drinking was a bad idea.

00:29:16.140 --> 00:29:20.710
But they mistakenly thought that
the average Stanford student actually

00:29:20.710 --> 00:29:22.050
was much more tolerant.

00:29:22.050 --> 00:29:23.860
Which was a complete myth.

00:29:23.860 --> 00:29:28.070
And the quickest way to solve that was to
provide each one of them feedback to show,

00:29:28.070 --> 00:29:33.920
hey, other Stanford students like you
also personally disapprove of drinking.

00:29:33.920 --> 00:29:38.100
And that actually led to a big
drop over the next six months

00:29:38.100 --> 00:29:41.450
in terms of drinking on the campus.

00:29:41.450 --> 00:29:45.490
But I want to pursue another interesting
image that your previous comment

00:29:45.490 --> 00:29:46.430
evoked for me, Bob.

00:29:47.530 --> 00:29:49.420
The image that your comment evoked for

00:29:49.420 --> 00:29:55.190
me is a small coterie of powerful
people thinking they know everything.

00:29:55.190 --> 00:29:58.360
Arrogance, or simply confidence.

00:29:58.360 --> 00:30:00.741
And then the rest of the organization,

00:30:00.741 --> 00:30:04.866
I sort of thought of them as more
Doubting Thomases in your imagery.

00:30:04.866 --> 00:30:09.730
So when is doubt a source of friction,
and how is that different from ignorance?

00:30:09.730 --> 00:30:12.040
Is it the same thing, or?

00:30:12.040 --> 00:30:13.710
&gt;&gt; Well to me ignorance

00:30:15.000 --> 00:30:19.860
is when you have the confidence
to go in and tell

00:30:19.860 --> 00:30:23.010
people what to do even though you don't
have any idea what the heck you're doing.

00:30:23.010 --> 00:30:25.610
Because you're in a powerful position and
you know better.

00:30:25.610 --> 00:30:27.720
And at Stanford just because,
I don't know,

00:30:27.720 --> 00:30:29.810
you're a world class electrical engineer.

00:30:29.810 --> 00:30:34.490
That means that therefore you're an expert
on organizational design or anything else.

00:30:34.490 --> 00:30:40.431
Which is this idea of the sort
of right stuff mentality.

00:30:40.431 --> 00:30:41.934
But doubt is not so bad.

00:30:41.934 --> 00:30:46.884
Because if it's doubt where people stop
and think about what they're going to

00:30:46.884 --> 00:30:51.909
do before they do it, because they think
they don't have enough information or

00:30:51.909 --> 00:30:55.816
there might be a problem,
to me that's constructive doubt.

00:30:55.816 --> 00:30:59.816
Because it's the kind of doubt that
Danny Kahneman talks about when you

00:30:59.816 --> 00:31:01.610
face an important decision.

00:31:01.610 --> 00:31:05.160
You slow down, and
instead of making a bunch of mistakes,

00:31:05.160 --> 00:31:09.510
you actually think about
what you're going to do.

00:31:09.510 --> 00:31:13.466
And we, a lot of us believe
in rapid prototyping and

00:31:13.466 --> 00:31:17.244
all that sort of stuff,
and to learn by failing.

00:31:17.244 --> 00:31:22.151
But the hallmark, to me, of people who
have the right amount of doubt and

00:31:22.151 --> 00:31:26.498
the proper amount of confidence
without being destructive is,

00:31:26.498 --> 00:31:31.488
what they do, is they find a little
place to do an experiment rather than to

00:31:31.488 --> 00:31:35.720
do something really stupid
that ruins everybody's life.

00:31:35.720 --> 00:31:37.030
And the example that I use, and

00:31:37.030 --> 00:31:40.129
I'm sure that many of you've been
involved in large IT rollouts.

00:31:41.420 --> 00:31:45.560
U Stanford, again, our history for
large IT rollouts is not too impressive.

00:31:45.560 --> 00:31:50.370
And one of the reasons that there's
problems with large IT rollouts is that

00:31:50.370 --> 00:31:53.610
some allegedly very smart people tend
to come up with the perfect solution.

00:31:53.610 --> 00:31:58.220
And then roll it out across campus,
and then their incompetence and

00:31:58.220 --> 00:32:02.060
overconfidence introduces massive
amount of friction and frustration and

00:32:02.060 --> 00:32:07.700
fatigue for literally thousands of people
who can't deal with their new IT system.

00:32:07.700 --> 00:32:12.660
And if instead they had done some
prototyping, some developing, being in

00:32:12.660 --> 00:32:18.400
a small sort of pilot are and really
really got it working, and then spread it.

00:32:18.400 --> 00:32:22.580
There would be much less friction for
everybody in the larger system.

00:32:23.590 --> 00:32:28.010
So that's where to me
actually having some doubt.

00:32:28.010 --> 00:32:29.990
That maybe it isn't right and

00:32:29.990 --> 00:32:34.360
maybe it isn't ready for a 100,000 or
10,000 people to use it.

00:32:34.360 --> 00:32:36.790
Is actually a proper
way to reduce friction.

00:32:36.790 --> 00:32:39.880
So but
when you get to the point where you're so

00:32:39.880 --> 00:32:43.410
insecure that you can't act,
that's a dangerous sort of doubt.

00:32:43.410 --> 00:32:46.100
&gt;&gt; I'd like to sort of quickly sort,
quick, yeah, more questions?

00:32:46.100 --> 00:32:48.730
&gt;&gt; I have one question
that I think is related.

00:32:48.730 --> 00:32:52.900
So Rudolfo Aguilar is asking,
what about trust and friction.

00:32:52.900 --> 00:32:54.790
Does lack of trust create friction?

00:32:55.950 --> 00:32:58.270
&gt;&gt; That's a great question, Rudolfo.

00:32:58.270 --> 00:32:59.740
&gt;&gt; Nice question Rudolfo.

00:32:59.740 --> 00:33:04.310
&gt;&gt; I think your question is a wonderful
sequel to the question on doubt.

00:33:04.310 --> 00:33:10.650
And what Bob was describing as
constructive doubt if you will.

00:33:10.650 --> 00:33:14.910
But the moment you use
the word distrust and

00:33:14.910 --> 00:33:19.780
I find that kind of interesting,
because distrust means suspicion.

00:33:19.780 --> 00:33:23.090
And suspicion essentially means one is

00:33:23.090 --> 00:33:24.860
questioning the motives
of the other person.

00:33:24.860 --> 00:33:29.630
And the moment that you begin to question
the motives of the other person,

00:33:29.630 --> 00:33:32.660
clearly the opportunity for
friction kind of multiplies.

00:33:33.780 --> 00:33:37.749
So a great example of friction
caused by distrust often is

00:33:37.749 --> 00:33:42.430
during the integration of new
acquisitions in an organization.

00:33:42.430 --> 00:33:47.900
I mean, what you actually have is
an enormous amount of friction then.

00:33:47.900 --> 00:33:51.220
In fact,
I would argue that if your company, for

00:33:51.220 --> 00:33:55.450
instance, is acquiring another company or
being merged with another company,

00:33:55.450 --> 00:34:01.330
a very useful way to reduce friction would
be right at the due diligent stage itself.

00:34:01.330 --> 00:34:04.960
Identify a series of hundred-day
improvement projects.

00:34:04.960 --> 00:34:09.580
We're going to bring in accounts
receivable from 15 million to 2 million.

00:34:09.580 --> 00:34:12.790
We're going to actually improve
the answer time on the phone calls

00:34:12.790 --> 00:34:17.060
when the customer counts come like five
seconds to two seconds or whatever.

00:34:17.060 --> 00:34:19.290
Obviously, I'm making up these numbers.

00:34:19.290 --> 00:34:24.720
What those kinds of projects do is
they actually give focus to people and

00:34:24.720 --> 00:34:27.700
they allow people for
both the acquirer and

00:34:27.700 --> 00:34:33.780
the acquiree to actually work together and
to overcome mistrust and distrust.

00:34:33.780 --> 00:34:35.440
So that's the advantage of doing that.

00:34:35.440 --> 00:34:40.660
So the short answer is distrust
indeed is a big cause of friction.

00:34:40.660 --> 00:34:42.050
&gt;&gt; The other thing, Rudolfo,

00:34:42.050 --> 00:34:47.380
I think you're really onto something
because the only thing I think about with

00:34:47.380 --> 00:34:53.090
distrust is with distrust comes suspicion
and actually justified paranoia.

00:34:53.090 --> 00:34:58.200
That you're so worried about being hurt,
about being surprised that you do a whole

00:34:58.200 --> 00:35:04.290
bunch of very, very time consuming,
sort of CYA activities and

00:35:04.290 --> 00:35:09.720
I even talk about this when I work
with people who have abusive bosses.

00:35:09.720 --> 00:35:14.220
I tell them to do a whole bunch of things
that add friction which is to document,

00:35:14.220 --> 00:35:16.780
to go around and check things off.

00:35:16.780 --> 00:35:21.670
To build a group of friends you don't
really need except to support you and

00:35:21.670 --> 00:35:26.310
I'm thinking even of some organizations
where there's really low trust cultures,

00:35:26.310 --> 00:35:28.310
including one that Huggy and
I worked with years ago.

00:35:28.310 --> 00:35:33.960
When I first met you, we were
talking to fairly senior executives.

00:35:33.960 --> 00:35:38.940
And they described how when there was
an important meeting, there always had

00:35:38.940 --> 00:35:43.980
to be seven or eight pre-meetings
that people have in smaller groups.

00:35:43.980 --> 00:35:49.700
To make sure that no one, and this is like
a relatively moderate size company but

00:35:49.700 --> 00:35:54.420
there is so much fear and paranoia even
in senior levels that you have seven or

00:35:54.420 --> 00:35:56.470
eight pre-meetings before
the real meetings.

00:35:56.470 --> 00:35:58.590
So there was no surprises so

00:35:58.590 --> 00:36:01.750
that you personally weren't in
a position to be hurt by others.

00:36:01.750 --> 00:36:04.320
So that you worked out
the politics before.

00:36:04.320 --> 00:36:10.100
And to me, that's a real sign of mistrust,
the fact that things have to be done

00:36:10.100 --> 00:36:15.250
backstage, and in fact,
I talked to a CEO about

00:36:15.250 --> 00:36:20.120
the most destructive members of his top
management team and I won't quite swear.

00:36:20.120 --> 00:36:23.100
He called them grim f-ers,
is what he called them.

00:36:23.100 --> 00:36:25.530
And these are people who in the meeting,
they really,

00:36:25.530 --> 00:36:28.670
really smile at you and
agree with everything you do.

00:36:28.670 --> 00:36:31.360
And then they run around and
stab you in the back.

00:36:31.360 --> 00:36:36.410
And he said when that happens, it just
creates a whole bunch of, not only fear,

00:36:36.410 --> 00:36:39.310
that's also another problem, but friction.

00:36:39.310 --> 00:36:41.750
It just adds all this
friction to the system.

00:36:41.750 --> 00:36:46.482
So I think that to your point,
I think that friction is a real,

00:36:46.482 --> 00:36:51.669
distrust, mistrust, paranoia,
which is sometimes justified,

00:36:51.669 --> 00:36:57.230
is a real strong sort of undercurrent
that causes these problems.

00:36:57.230 --> 00:36:58.490
because people have to protect themselves.

00:36:58.490 --> 00:36:59.320
It's not irrational.

00:37:00.480 --> 00:37:03.850
&gt;&gt; But listen to what's also
interesting is the confusion also

00:37:03.850 --> 00:37:08.240
is a consequence of distrust because you
never know why something happens or not.

00:37:08.240 --> 00:37:14.190
Quickly summarizing, we've actually
identified a number of sources of drag and

00:37:14.190 --> 00:37:15.570
friction in organizations.

00:37:15.570 --> 00:37:17.100
One was incentives.

00:37:17.100 --> 00:37:18.190
Another was actually rules.

00:37:18.190 --> 00:37:24.080
The third was inequality or
power differences, that Bob alluded to.

00:37:24.080 --> 00:37:27.180
The fourth, of course, is distrust.

00:37:27.180 --> 00:37:30.740
And one takeaway for all of you,
thanks to Karen's question,

00:37:30.740 --> 00:37:37.080
is remember, don't go back and
put more burdens on your extra milers.

00:37:37.080 --> 00:37:39.230
These are the people helping you,

00:37:39.230 --> 00:37:42.220
don't shoot yourself in the foot
by giving them more to do.

00:37:42.220 --> 00:37:44.900
Because you're going to frustrate them,
they're going to be fatigued,

00:37:44.900 --> 00:37:48.450
they're going to leave the organization,
you'll damage yourself.

00:37:48.450 --> 00:37:50.210
We have a little time and

00:37:50.210 --> 00:37:54.430
I want us to kind of quickly
investigate another possibility.

00:37:54.430 --> 00:37:56.050
Is friction always bad?

00:37:56.050 --> 00:37:59.480
Are there times when we should
create friction in organizations?

00:37:59.480 --> 00:38:00.840
What do you think Bob?

00:38:00.840 --> 00:38:05.840
&gt;&gt; Well I think friction is good,
under some circumstances.

00:38:05.840 --> 00:38:10.740
One is, there are some situations
which are really big situations.

00:38:10.740 --> 00:38:13.700
But things like an acquisition decision.

00:38:13.700 --> 00:38:15.550
Whether or not to lay off people.

00:38:15.550 --> 00:38:18.610
Having some process where people

00:38:18.610 --> 00:38:22.910
are actually forced to think
about what they're doing.

00:38:22.910 --> 00:38:26.680
And there's also another
reason where something

00:38:26.680 --> 00:38:29.510
that might feel like
friction might be important.

00:38:29.510 --> 00:38:34.410
Which is that sometimes to do things
with enough humanity so it doesn't

00:38:34.410 --> 00:38:39.445
hurt people and it actually fuels
things like trust in the organization.

00:38:39.445 --> 00:38:44.620
You’ve got to sort of slow down and keep
things in a somewhat less efficient way.

00:38:44.620 --> 00:38:48.210
One of the classic thing for
the back to the **** question.

00:38:48.210 --> 00:38:50.820
I've got, actually I get this once a year.

00:38:50.820 --> 00:38:54.390
Somebody works for a company and
somebody in HR wants to fire

00:38:54.390 --> 00:38:58.290
people by sending them emails or
text, rather than doing it in person.

00:38:58.290 --> 00:38:59.740
And it's more efficient.

00:38:59.740 --> 00:39:01.620
It gets rid of friction.

00:39:01.620 --> 00:39:05.250
But if you want to create
fear in an organization.

00:39:05.250 --> 00:39:07.810
And, large scale cases, RadioShack,

00:39:07.810 --> 00:39:11.300
at one point, fired huge amounts
of people by sending them e-mails.

00:39:11.300 --> 00:39:15.910
So, maybe this might add friction, but

00:39:15.910 --> 00:39:20.120
I think or get rid of friction, but
it creates so many other ripple effects.

00:39:20.120 --> 00:39:24.820
This also, just a little design thing and
then we're stop.

00:39:24.820 --> 00:39:28.507
A friend of ours, Michael During,
who was head of Ebay North America,

00:39:28.507 --> 00:39:30.233
he's now a venture capitalist.

00:39:30.233 --> 00:39:32.939
He used to make this argument,
that in the early days of eBay,

00:39:32.939 --> 00:39:34.830
I don't know what's going on in eBay now.

00:39:34.830 --> 00:39:40.854
There was a big argument between the pure
IT type user design folks who just wanted

00:39:40.854 --> 00:39:47.140
everything to be as easy as possible to
get in and out of eBay with your purchase.

00:39:47.140 --> 00:39:48.890
But Michael's an old retailer.

00:39:48.890 --> 00:39:51.580
Actually ran a huge retail
store when he was in his 20's.

00:39:51.580 --> 00:39:56.400
And he said the way retail works is,
we like to sort of slow things down.

00:39:56.400 --> 00:39:59.160
And show things to people along the way.

00:39:59.160 --> 00:40:01.000
That because they might buy them and
slow them down.

00:40:01.000 --> 00:40:04.020
And if you've been to IKEA and
I'm sure many of you know IKEA.

00:40:04.020 --> 00:40:06.250
That's just the whole theory of the store.

00:40:06.250 --> 00:40:07.800
It takes forever to get out of the store.

00:40:07.800 --> 00:40:09.470
And they just show you all this crap.

00:40:09.470 --> 00:40:12.090
And you throw all this stuff in your
cart that you maybe didn't want in

00:40:12.090 --> 00:40:13.306
the first place.

00:40:13.306 --> 00:40:16.750
So there might be times when people,
at least for capitalistic reasons,

00:40:16.750 --> 00:40:19.859
you want them to slow down, and
also human and decision-making,

00:40:19.859 --> 00:40:21.710
those are the things that come to mind.

00:40:21.710 --> 00:40:25.658
&gt;&gt; Yeah, those are very nice examples,
Bob, I want to pick up on the IKEA one,

00:40:25.658 --> 00:40:26.170
as well.

00:40:26.170 --> 00:40:31.029
What I heard Bob saying, hey,
you might really need friction

00:40:31.029 --> 00:40:35.060
when you actually want curiosity and
commitment.

00:40:35.060 --> 00:40:39.718
&gt;&gt; Now that's interesting.

00:40:39.718 --> 00:40:43.050
&gt;&gt; That's kind of what I got
from what you just said.

00:40:43.050 --> 00:40:47.080
And the IKEA experience
is an interesting one.

00:40:47.080 --> 00:40:50.640
Because in marketing there's this
thing called the IKEA effect.

00:40:50.640 --> 00:40:54.910
In this study, what they showed was if
you ask somebody to pre-assemble some

00:40:54.910 --> 00:40:59.193
kind of shelf or whatever it is for you,
as opposed to you making it yourself.

00:40:59.193 --> 00:41:01.070
People who made it themselves and

00:41:01.070 --> 00:41:06.390
experienced friction were actually more
committed and were much more satisfied.

00:41:06.390 --> 00:41:08.024
The people who got something made,

00:41:08.024 --> 00:41:10.430
they only kind of were able
to identify the defects.

00:41:10.430 --> 00:41:13.290
That wasn't done properly,
this wasn't done properly.

00:41:13.290 --> 00:41:16.991
They were still in the consumer mode,
and not in the producer mode.

00:41:16.991 --> 00:41:18.406
I'm kind of wondering,

00:41:18.406 --> 00:41:23.086
should organizations actually create
friction right at the stage of hiring?

00:41:23.086 --> 00:41:24.839
&gt;&gt; Well, that's kind of interesting,

00:41:24.839 --> 00:41:27.798
because you're making me think
this is a double-edged sword,

00:41:27.798 --> 00:41:31.440
because a lot of what Huggy's talking
about is the psychology of commitment.

00:41:31.440 --> 00:41:35.017
That a lot of organizations
make it difficult to be hired,

00:41:35.017 --> 00:41:39.397
to sort of run you sort of a gauntlet
in the early days in the organization

00:41:39.397 --> 00:41:42.110
because it actually does
increase commitment.

00:41:42.110 --> 00:41:44.090
So the friction increases commitment.

00:41:44.090 --> 00:41:48.557
But the thing I don't like about that is
that you have a bunch of people who went

00:41:48.557 --> 00:41:53.441
through a very difficult, high-friction
experience, and they feel obligated,

00:41:53.441 --> 00:41:55.526
I did it, so I'm going to do it to you.

00:41:55.526 --> 00:41:59.150
And so you end up with this sort of-
&gt;&gt; Vicious circle.

00:41:59.150 --> 00:42:01.890
&gt;&gt; Sort of vicious circle,
where everybody adds more and

00:42:01.890 --> 00:42:04.095
more friction, and in some ways-
&gt;&gt; That's really nice.

00:42:04.095 --> 00:42:06.260
&gt;&gt; It isn't just friction, it's load.

00:42:06.260 --> 00:42:10.724
Where you actually see this in a specific
case, as many may know, that for

00:42:10.724 --> 00:42:13.532
medical residency, surgical residencies,

00:42:13.532 --> 00:42:17.431
and so on, that the residents
tend to work 90 plus hour weeks.

00:42:17.431 --> 00:42:21.558
And it causes all of these physical and
mental health problems and

00:42:21.558 --> 00:42:26.073
is really hard on the profession, and
they keep trying to get rid of it.

00:42:26.073 --> 00:42:30.276
And they actually haven't been very
effective at getting rid of it in

00:42:30.276 --> 00:42:34.767
part because the residents who
are now doctors, attending physicians,

00:42:34.767 --> 00:42:38.280
and so on, they say I did it and
I'm going to do it to you.

00:42:38.280 --> 00:42:43.425
And so there is that sort of,
in this case, cycle of friction and

00:42:43.425 --> 00:42:47.450
abuse and load, that the prior
generations do on the new generations.

00:42:47.450 --> 00:42:48.440
Because they did it and

00:42:48.440 --> 00:42:50.130
they want to maintain the-
&gt;&gt; This is a-

00:42:50.130 --> 00:42:51.230
&gt;&gt; Justification-

00:42:51.230 --> 00:42:54.200
&gt;&gt; This is a really fascinating idea Bob.

00:42:54.200 --> 00:42:59.900
The reason I find myself fascinated by
your argument is, this happens when we,

00:42:59.900 --> 00:43:04.335
of course, have these conversations,
I do get fascinated.

00:43:04.335 --> 00:43:07.985
But what you just said
when you were saying this,

00:43:07.985 --> 00:43:12.370
it reminded me of Dale Miller's
work on moral licensing.

00:43:12.370 --> 00:43:17.042
And Dale's argument when he does
this experiments on moral licensing,

00:43:17.042 --> 00:43:22.094
is that if you do a good deed, that is,
you donate 50 bucks to a non-profit,

00:43:22.094 --> 00:43:25.081
you are more likely to run
through a red light or

00:43:25.081 --> 00:43:28.479
a stop sign because you
think you're a good person.

00:43:28.479 --> 00:43:29.430
&gt;&gt; You're a good person, yeah.

00:43:29.430 --> 00:43:31.793
&gt;&gt; Then you license
yourself to do bad things.

00:43:31.793 --> 00:43:34.611
But just in this lovely
account that you gave me,

00:43:34.611 --> 00:43:37.030
I never sort of thought about it that way.

00:43:37.030 --> 00:43:40.270
It's sort of like if you've
been a recipient of friction,

00:43:40.270 --> 00:43:44.660
you're going to be more morally
licensed to impose it on other people.

00:43:44.660 --> 00:43:49.590
I find that really quite-
&gt;&gt; It's justifying your identity

00:43:49.590 --> 00:43:50.220
and existence.

00:43:50.220 --> 00:43:52.477
&gt;&gt; Right,
that's actually very interesting.

00:43:52.477 --> 00:43:55.540
&gt;&gt; I'm going to ask Bob and
Huggy to take a few more questions.

00:43:56.550 --> 00:44:02.134
So one question that we had come up
from a few different participants is,

00:44:02.134 --> 00:44:08.010
does a small company necessarily have
less friction than a large company?

00:44:08.010 --> 00:44:11.210
What is the relationship between
the size of the company and

00:44:11.210 --> 00:44:13.130
the amount of friction within it?

00:44:13.130 --> 00:44:15.230
&gt;&gt; That's a great question.

00:44:15.230 --> 00:44:18.050
Here's my quick, sort of, take on it.

00:44:20.010 --> 00:44:23.960
I think it was in one of the programs
that maybe Bob and I were running,

00:44:23.960 --> 00:44:26.370
or I forget when I met this executive.

00:44:28.120 --> 00:44:31.740
I asked this person a simple question.

00:44:31.740 --> 00:44:34.800
I said what's the difference between
a startup and a large company?

00:44:35.870 --> 00:44:38.710
And here was this person's answer.

00:44:38.710 --> 00:44:43.330
He came back and he said well, professor,
he said small companies have big people,

00:44:44.580 --> 00:44:48.640
and the larger the company
the smaller the person, and

00:44:48.640 --> 00:44:50.490
that hopefully gives you an idea.

00:44:50.490 --> 00:44:53.340
It's not that there's no
friction in a startup,

00:44:53.340 --> 00:44:55.780
there is indeed friction in a startup.

00:44:55.780 --> 00:44:58.922
But what you actually have is
this sense of mission analogous,

00:44:58.922 --> 00:45:00.118
which overrides that.

00:45:00.118 --> 00:45:04.545
My nephew works in a startup, and
I won't mention the name of the startup.

00:45:04.545 --> 00:45:06.116
I ask him, what's it like?

00:45:06.116 --> 00:45:08.527
He told me,
he said it's like a labor camp.

00:45:08.527 --> 00:45:11.308
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Working all the time!

00:45:11.308 --> 00:45:16.631
So there is a lot of friction there too,
but people don't feel as frustrated and

00:45:16.631 --> 00:45:20.615
as fatigued as in a larger corporation,
would be my wager.

00:45:20.615 --> 00:45:24.616
&gt;&gt; So to piggyback on what Huggy said,
I think the evidence says, and

00:45:24.616 --> 00:45:27.402
back to Michael Dearing I
already mentioned,

00:45:27.402 --> 00:45:31.331
who moved from places like eBay and
the like to now he's done early

00:45:31.331 --> 00:45:35.920
stage financing for more than
a hundred startups, very successful.

00:45:35.920 --> 00:45:41.339
And he'll argue that speed is the only
real advantage that startups have.

00:45:41.339 --> 00:45:46.505
But I would sort of add as
a caveat that the worst situations

00:45:46.505 --> 00:45:54.210
are when companies use to be startups and
they get bigger and bigger and bigger.

00:45:54.210 --> 00:45:58.650
And management is in denial of the fact
they need some control mechanisms,

00:45:58.650 --> 00:46:00.150
they need some hierarchy.

00:46:00.150 --> 00:46:03.420
They need some process,
some specialized roles.

00:46:03.420 --> 00:46:06.045
And they don't accept
that they've grown up and

00:46:06.045 --> 00:46:09.257
have to deal with the fact
they're a larger organization.

00:46:09.257 --> 00:46:13.882
And it's actually, interesting example,
GitHub, which is now doing incredibly

00:46:13.882 --> 00:46:17.852
well, went through a period where they
just wanted to be a big startup and

00:46:17.852 --> 00:46:21.365
not have any controls and
let everybody do what they wanted, and

00:46:21.365 --> 00:46:23.210
it led to some pretty ugly stuff.

00:46:23.210 --> 00:46:28.050
They got rid of one of their CEOs and
brought up another guy who's much better.

00:46:28.050 --> 00:46:32.519
And the first thing he did was to
start a process of actually adding big

00:46:32.519 --> 00:46:34.293
company-like controls,

00:46:34.293 --> 00:46:39.087
because they became big enough that
acting like a startup didn't work at all.

00:46:39.087 --> 00:46:43.274
So, yes, startups have less friction
than large organizations, but

00:46:43.274 --> 00:46:46.990
the worst situation is when you
pretend you haven't grown up.

00:46:48.620 --> 00:46:52.123
&gt;&gt; So another question that we have
received is, can you share, and

00:46:52.123 --> 00:46:55.946
I think you've shared a few examples
during the webinar, but are there

00:46:55.946 --> 00:46:59.770
really good examples of companies who
have been able to create a culture

00:46:59.770 --> 00:47:04.132
that has less friction, or have been able
to very effectively reduce friction?

00:47:07.719 --> 00:47:09.274
&gt;&gt; A good example that Bob and

00:47:09.274 --> 00:47:14.156
I wrote a case study about was Adobe where
they actually dropped their performance

00:47:14.156 --> 00:47:19.930
appraisal system because they found out
they were spending 90,000 manager hours.

00:47:19.930 --> 00:47:21.730
They weren't getting anything.

00:47:21.730 --> 00:47:27.870
So they certainly have taken friction out,
but at the same time, given that they have

00:47:27.870 --> 00:47:33.260
yanked the system out, people are actually
yearning for, what do we do instead?

00:47:33.260 --> 00:47:36.010
Can we actually develop
something much more lightweight?

00:47:37.190 --> 00:47:42.440
The other solution I would recommend
to people is be mischievous.

00:47:42.440 --> 00:47:44.880
Think of having the words like MVP for

00:47:44.880 --> 00:47:49.297
the person who copies everybody
on every email that they receive.

00:47:49.297 --> 00:47:51.723
Nobody would want to-
&gt;&gt; A little friendly humiliation?

00:47:51.723 --> 00:47:54.099
&gt;&gt; A little friendly
humiliation is a good thing.

00:47:54.099 --> 00:47:58.350
Or MVP, the person who attends
the most number of meetings.

00:47:58.350 --> 00:48:04.430
Things like that, you can actually be
a little edgy and you can do that as well.

00:48:04.430 --> 00:48:06.042
So that's another thing to do.

00:48:06.042 --> 00:48:11.042
But the most important thing
is before you do anything, you

00:48:11.042 --> 00:48:18.370
might really want to ask yourself or Pose
the friction and drag test for yourself.

00:48:18.370 --> 00:48:22.540
How is what I'm doing,
how is that causing fraction.

00:48:22.540 --> 00:48:25.740
So, let me close with
one quick example and

00:48:25.740 --> 00:48:29.170
this was an example of a large
multinational company.

00:48:29.170 --> 00:48:34.264
They wanted to reduce costs and
the person who didn't think much about

00:48:34.264 --> 00:48:39.183
friction quickly pulled up an Excel
spreadsheet and said, my God,

00:48:39.183 --> 00:48:44.039
our sales guys in Germany all have
Mercedes sedans leased to them.

00:48:44.039 --> 00:48:49.519
We can reduce costs by giving all of
them cheaper Volkswagens instead.

00:48:49.519 --> 00:48:51.460
They partly reduced costs.

00:48:51.460 --> 00:48:55.319
But later when this executive flew to
Germany, one of the sales people told him,

00:48:55.319 --> 00:48:56.527
do you know what you did?

00:48:56.527 --> 00:49:00.559
And the person said, well, we just
reduced cost, we took the Mercedes out,

00:49:00.559 --> 00:49:01.438
we gave you a VW.

00:49:01.438 --> 00:49:04.090
He said,
you have no idea what you've done.

00:49:04.090 --> 00:49:08.430
He said, look at me, I'm a salesperson,
I drive home in the VW.

00:49:08.430 --> 00:49:11.700
My spouse looks at me and
thinks, my god he's in trouble.

00:49:11.700 --> 00:49:15.960
The children kind of ask me,
dad what happened to the Mercedes?

00:49:15.960 --> 00:49:20.320
My neighbors begin to give us strange
looks thinking is the family in trouble

00:49:20.320 --> 00:49:21.670
at the firm.

00:49:21.670 --> 00:49:24.722
And he looked at this executive and
said that as a sales person,

00:49:24.722 --> 00:49:27.110
what's the most important thing I need?

00:49:27.110 --> 00:49:29.420
And this executive said, confidence.

00:49:29.420 --> 00:49:32.586
And then the sales person said, what have
you done with my confidence with this?

00:49:32.586 --> 00:49:34.322
You've actually lowered it.

00:49:34.322 --> 00:49:39.305
To this executive's credit, he realized
it and then discovered that the bottom of

00:49:39.305 --> 00:49:44.250
the company in Texas, and there were
a lot of smokers in that company.

00:49:44.250 --> 00:49:49.391
And that's when he realized that the
better to launch an anti-smoking campaign

00:49:49.391 --> 00:49:54.800
there and reduce the insurance
premium rather than yank the car out.

00:49:54.800 --> 00:49:57.680
So, it's a good idea
before you do anything ask

00:49:57.680 --> 00:50:00.780
yourselves how much friction
am I going to create.

00:50:00.780 --> 00:50:05.209
And to Bob's point, some of the friction
that you create may not be visible

00:50:05.209 --> 00:50:07.122
to you when you make a decision.

00:50:07.122 --> 00:50:10.630
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so
I would add just one little thing.

00:50:10.630 --> 00:50:14.530
This company is actually cut,
I think of like Amazon and Walmart.

00:50:14.530 --> 00:50:18.670
Which are not the nicest companies to
their employees in the world, by the way.

00:50:18.670 --> 00:50:20.928
And I've worked for both of them.

00:50:20.928 --> 00:50:27.012
So, I'm biting some hands that feed me,
but that said, they are organizations

00:50:27.012 --> 00:50:32.922
where really there is a focus on both
frugality and making things easier to do.

00:50:32.922 --> 00:50:37.101
And then I would just add just a word
from someone who teaches in the SI&amp;E

00:50:37.101 --> 00:50:41.443
program who's one of my charismatic
heroes the young Jeremy Utley.

00:50:41.443 --> 00:50:43.195
What's the name of the course he teaches?

00:50:43.195 --> 00:50:44.225
&gt;&gt; Empathize and prototype.

00:50:44.225 --> 00:50:45.245
&gt;&gt; Empathize and prototype.

00:50:45.245 --> 00:50:47.725
The fact that he says and
I teach with Jeremy and

00:50:47.725 --> 00:50:50.595
Jeremy is the perfect friction reducer.

00:50:50.595 --> 00:50:53.265
One of his motto's whenever
we're planning lessons or

00:50:53.265 --> 00:50:57.385
thinking of anything is I hate
wasting other people's time.

00:50:57.385 --> 00:50:59.085
And I think that-
&gt;&gt; There you go.

00:50:59.085 --> 00:51:01.576
&gt;&gt; I see that in how he makes decisions.

00:51:01.576 --> 00:51:05.825
In all sorts of ways, always thinking of
how can I make things less burdensome for

00:51:05.825 --> 00:51:09.715
my students, for the executives I lead,
for the companies I consult to?

00:51:09.715 --> 00:51:14.332
And I think ending on Jeremy's motto and
just to really hate and

00:51:14.332 --> 00:51:19.298
try to figure it out, how not to
be disrespectful of other people's

00:51:19.298 --> 00:51:22.190
time might be a good motto for all of us.

00:51:22.190 --> 00:51:24.740
&gt;&gt; With that I want to really
thank both Huggy and Bob.

00:51:24.740 --> 00:51:27.154
I am very much hoping that
you enjoyed this hour and

00:51:27.154 --> 00:51:29.352
that we didn't waste your time and
our time.

00:51:29.352 --> 00:51:33.505
[LAUGH] And that you learned
about some valuable insights and

00:51:33.505 --> 00:51:38.752
I encourage you to check out any of
